+### Why a picker page comes from each repository's newest pages and stops at four
+
+Gitea has no search that names a set of repositories, so the only way to merge them is to read each repository's list and order the union by activity. Four pages per repository (200 items at the 50-item cap) bound one page's cost at `folders × 4` list reads; scanning deeper would read older items nobody asked for, and the list's "more" ends at the cap instead of pretending the folder holds nothing older. A single search cursor like GitHub's does not exist here, so the cursor carries the offset plus a hash of the scope; when the scope changed, the offset would index another result set, so the list starts again.
+
+### Why per-repository pages are cached for 30 s and skipped on a forced read
+
+Paging a merged list re-reads every repository for each page; without the cache the second page of ten repositories costs ten list reads again. Thirty seconds matches the shell's own list reads, so a page is never older than the list it extends. The board's list refresh sends `refresh: true`, which skips the cache, so a change made on the instance shows on a refresh instead of waiting out the TTL.
+
